<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Crisis · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Suez Crisis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1956">1956</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Suez Crisis was the international crisis of 1956 in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and Israel invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Gamal Abdel Nasser</a> nationalised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a>. Under pressure from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, the invaders withdrew. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> treat it as a humiliation that showed Britain could no longer act as an independent imperial power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suez-crisis.jpg" alt="Suez Crisis" width="320" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fleet Air Arm official photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Port_Said_from_air.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
